  scenario = $qb$A 40-year-old male is recovering from abdominal surgery and is receiving morphine via a PCA pump. He reports nausea and rates his pain as 4/10, then drifts back to sleep mid-sentence. His respiratory rate is 10/min. Naloxone is available per the unit opioid-reversal protocol.$qb$,
  options = $qb${"options": ["Administer the prescribed antiemetic for nausea.", "Encourage the client to use the PCA pump for pain control.", "Stop the PCA and administer naloxone per protocol, then notify the healthcare provider.", "Increase the PCA pump dose to manage pain more effectively."], "distractorRationale": {"Administer the prescribed antiemetic for nausea.": "Incorrect: Nausea is not the danger; many antiemetics add sedation, and RR 10/min must be treated first.", "Encourage the client to use the PCA pump for pain control.": "Incorrect: More morphine would deepen the respiratory depression; his pain is only 4/10.", "Stop the PCA and administer naloxone per protocol, then notify the healthcare provider.": "Correct: He falls asleep mid-sentence with a respiratory rate of 10/min on a morphine PCA, which is opioid-induced respiratory depression. The nurse stops the opioid source, gives naloxone per the unit protocol, and then notifies the provider.", "Increase the PCA pump dose to manage pain more effectively.": "Incorrect: A higher dose worsens respiratory depression when RR is already 10/min."}, "clinicalReasoning": "Recognize and Analyze Cues: He falls asleep mid-sentence with a respiratory rate of 10/min on a morphine PCA, which is opioid-induced respiratory depression. The nurse stops the opioid source, gives naloxone per the unit protocol, and then notifies the provider. Take Action: Stop the PCA and administer naloxone per protocol, then notify the healthcare provider.", "kind": "mcq", "blueprintTopic": "pain-opioids-nsaids", "blueprintSystem": "nclex-physiological", "generationMeta": {"model": "gpt-4o", "batchId": "nclex-gap-2026-07-06-g19se3", "qcScore": 0.9200000000000002, "slotIndex": 69, "examNumber": 9008, "generatedAt": "2026-07-06T06:39:49.075Z", "pipelineVersion": "gpt-4o-nclex-full-exam-v2"}, "clientNeedsCategory": "Pharmacological Therapies", "reviewModuleSlug": "prioritization", "memoryCardIds": ["nclex-pri-abc-first", "nclex-pri-see-first", "nclex-pri-assess-act", "nclex-pri-trap-eventually"], "top500Drugs": ["Morphine (MS Contin)"], "structureIds": ["heart", "kidneys", "spleen"]}$qb$,
  "correctAnswer" = $qb$Stop the PCA and administer naloxone per protocol, then notify the healthcare provider.$qb$,
  explanation = $qb$C (Stop the PCA and administer naloxone per protocol, then notify the healthcare provider.) is correct. He falls asleep mid-sentence with a respiratory rate of 10/min on a morphine PCA, which is opioid-induced respiratory depression. The nurse stops the opioid source, gives naloxone per the unit protocol, and then notifies the provider. "Administer the prescribed antiemetic for nausea." is wrong: Nausea is not the danger; many antiemetics add sedation, and RR 10/min must be treated first. "Encourage the client to use the PCA pump for pain control." is wrong: More morphine would deepen the respiratory depression; his pain is only 4/10. "Increase the PCA pump dose to manage pain more effectively." is wrong: A higher dose worsens respiratory depression when RR is already 10/min.$qb$,
